# Commit After Changes

After every significant code change (new module, feature, refactor, bug fix, or completing a phase), create a git commit immediately. Do not accumulate large uncommitted changes.

## When to Commit

- After completing a phase or milestone
- After adding a new module or feature
- After a refactor that touches multiple files
- After fixing a bug (include the regression test in the same commit)
- After adding or updating tests for existing code

## Commit Checklist

1. Run tests (`pytest`) — all must pass
2. Run linter (`ruff check`) — no errors
3. Stage relevant files
4. Write a Conventional Commit message (see 090-git-conventions)
5. Verify clean working tree after commit
